 ### [Bottom-up Neighbor-Finding Ray Traversal](5610-bottom-up-neighbor-finding-ray-traversal.md)
-A method for intersecting rays with an octree by determining the sequence of cells via neighbor lookups rather than hierarchical descent. It solves the problem of efficiently stepping from one occupied cell to the next, avoiding the overhead of traversing empty space from the root. Unlike top-down methods, it starts at the first intersected cell and moves laterally or vertically through the tree structure.
+Bottom-up neighbor-finding ray traversal is a method for intersecting rays with an octree-represented scene by determining the sequence of cells the ray passes through via neighbor lookups rather than hierarchical descent. It solves the problem of efficiently stepping from one occupied or relevant cell to the next along a ray path, avoiding the overhead of traversing empty space from the root. Unlike top-down traversal methods that descend from the root for every step, this approach starts at the first intersected cell and moves laterally or vertically through the tree structure to find the subsequent cell, offering a trade-off between implementation complexity and traversal speed depending on the density of the scene. [Bottom-up Neighbor-Finding Ray Traversal](5610-bottom-up-neighbor-finding-ray-traversal.md)
 
 ### [Octree-based Raycasting](5707-octree-based-raycasting.md)
-A volume rendering technique that accelerates ray traversal by skipping empty space using an octree. It reduces traversal cost from $O(n)$ to depend only on occupied space. It differs from [Ray-Occlusion Traversal in Octree](5121-ray-occlusion-traversal-in-octree.md) by explicitly managing dynamic voxel updates and concurrency, and from [Bottom-up Neighbor-Finding Ray Traversal](5610-bottom-up-neighbor-finding-ray-traversal.md) by relying on hierarchical empty-space skipping.
+Octree-based raycasting is a volume rendering technique that accelerates ray traversal by utilizing an octree structure to skip empty space regions. It solves the performance bottleneck of naive raycasting, which scales linearly with the number of voxels $O(n)$, by culling large volumes of empty space. It differs from its parent [Ray-Occlusion Traversal in Octree](5121-ray-occlusion-traversal-in-octree.md) by explicitly maintaining voxel counts per node to support dynamic updates, and from [Bottom-up Neighbor-Finding Ray Traversal](5610-bottom-up-neighbor-finding-ray-traversal.md) by relying on top-down parametric analysis rather than neighbor-finding for traversal. [Octree-based Raycasting](5707-octree-based-raycasting.md)
 
